# OpenRung broker configuration. Copy to .env and edit, then:
#   docker compose up -d --build
#
# The broker is configured entirely through OPENRUNG_* environment variables.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared bearer token that volunteer-class relays and hubs must present to
# register. It gates the relay directory that clients route their VPN traffic
# through.
#
# The broker refuses to start without a token, so this template opts into an
# open, unauthenticated broker (anyone can register a relay). This is a
# deliberate choice: OpenRung's public network intentionally runs open so any
# volunteer can contribute a relay without a shared secret. To require auth
# instead (e.g. a private deployment), set OPENRUNG_VOLUNTEER_TOKEN below — a
# token supersedes the anonymous flag, which then becomes a no-op — and remove
# or comment out the anonymous line.
#
# Keep this line HERE, in the env file loaded via --env-file, not as an ad-hoc
# `docker run -e OPENRUNG_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS_REGISTRATION=true`. The broker fails
# closed at startup, so a redeploy that forgets an inline -e flag crash-loops;
# in the env file the opt-in travels with every recreate.
OPENRUNG_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS_REGISTRATION=true
#OPENRUNG_VOLUNTEER_TOKEN=

# Privileged token that authorizes node_class=foundation registrations, so
# clients can tell foundation-operated relays from community volunteers in the
# signed relay list. Give it ONLY to relays the foundation runs itself — never
# to volunteers or community hubs — and generate it independently of the
# volunteer token (the broker refuses to start when the two are equal, since a
# shared value would let any volunteer self-label as foundation). Unset
# disables foundation registration; anonymous/volunteer registration is
# unaffected either way.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Keep the populated env file root-owned and mode 0600. On cloud hosts, transfer
# this value only after boot over an authenticated channel; never put it in
# cloud-init/user-data, provider metadata, inline docker -e arguments, or traced
# shell commands. Recreate the container with --env-file after changing it — a
# restart does not reload container environment variables.
#OPENRUNG_FOUNDATION_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relay-list signing (required)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Standard base64 of the 32-byte Ed25519 seed that signs every relay-list
# response (detached signature in the X-OpenRung-Relays-Signature header), so
# clients can verify the directory over non-TLS channels. The broker refuses to
# start without it — serving unsigned lists would look healthy while every
# verifying client rejected discovery. Generate a seed with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Clients pin the matching public keys, so production must use the operator's
# active seed. Keep this file 0600 once a real seed is in it; NEVER commit one.
#OPENRUNG_RELAY_SIGNING_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relay-bound WSS ticket signing (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dedicated standard-base64 32-byte Ed25519 seed for short-lived WSS session
# tickets. Keep it distinct from OPENRUNG_RELAY_SIGNING_KEY. When unset, the
# ticket endpoint is disabled and registrations that advertise wss_fronts are
# rejected; direct Reality remains available.
#
# Generate and store this exactly like the relay-list seed:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Never copy this private seed to a relay. Relay-local sidecars receive only the
# derived public key; see deploy/broker/README.md for the handoff and overlap
# rotation procedure. Keep this file 0600 and NEVER commit a populated value.
#OPENRUNG_WSS_TICKET_SIGNING_SEED=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client user authentication (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Standard-base64 32-byte HMAC key behind client access tokens. Setting it
# enables POST /api/v1/users/challenge and /api/v1/users/auth, plus the
# operational user routes when OPENRUNG_API_TOKEN is set. Only the broker
# reads it; rotating it invalidates every outstanding access token at once.
# Generate it like the seeds above (openssl rand -base64 32), keep it distinct
# from every other credential, keep this file 0600 and NEVER commit a value.
#OPENRUNG_USER_TOKEN_KEY=
# Access token lifetime (1m–168h) and the per-source budget of NEW user
# identities (IPv6 sources are grouped by /64). Negative limits disable a
# window. Defaults shown.
#OPENRUNG_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=24h
#OPENRUNG_ENROLLMENT_BURST_LIMIT=3
#OPENRUNG_ENROLLMENT_DAILY_LIMIT=10
# Refuse NEW user identities from datacenter/hosting networks (existing ones
# authenticate from anywhere). Both files come from refresh-source-asn.sh on
# the host (see README.md); mount their directory read-only into the
# container. Unset = off. New enrollments get a retryable 503 while the table
# is missing or older than the max age. Exempt CIDRs are never refused.
#OPENRUNG_SOURCE_ASN_DB=/var/lib/openrung-source/ip2asn.tsv
#OPENRUNG_DATACENTER_ASN_LIST=/var/lib/openrung-source/datacenter-asns.txt
#OPENRUNG_SOURCE_ASN_MAX_AGE=168h
#OPENRUNG_ENROLLMENT_EXEMPT_CIDRS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telemetry dashboard (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set a long random token to enable the protected /admin/dashboard dashboard.
# When unset, the dashboard and its data API return 404. Serve the broker over
# HTTPS (e.g. behind Cloudflare) so the admin session cookie is protected.
#OPENRUNG_DASHBOARD_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operational API (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bearer token for the machine-to-machine fleet endpoint
#   GET /admin/api/relays/inventory
# which returns EVERY currently active relay (never the ranked, truncated page
# clients receive) in stable relay-ID order, signed like the relay list. Meant
# for the operator's own tooling: fleet audits, deployment checks, capacity
# reports. When unset the route is not registered and returns 404.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Generate it independently of every other credential here — the broker refuses
# to start when it equals the volunteer, foundation, or dashboard token, or
# either signing seed. Store it exactly like the foundation token: keep this
# file root-owned and mode 0600, transfer the value only after boot over an
# authenticated channel, and never put it in cloud-init/user-data, provider
# metadata, inline docker -e arguments, or traced shell commands. Recreate the
# container with --env-file after changing it — a restart does not reload
# container environment variables.
#OPENRUNG_API_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Listen address (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# With host networking (the compose default) this binds directly on the host.
#OPENRUNG_ADDR=:8080

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trusted proxies (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The only peers whose CF-Connecting-IP / X-Forwarded-For the broker trusts for
# the real client IP; none by default. List the local TLS terminator (the
# production Caddy on loopback) or another proxy you operate, comma-separated.
# Do NOT add broad or shared ranges — any peer inside them can spoof the source
# IP past rate limiting.
#OPENRUNG_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=127.0.0.1/32,::1/128

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Abuse budgets (optional; unset keeps the built-in defaults)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-client token buckets as <per-second rate>:<burst>, e.g. 0.5:10. Set the
# production values here on the broker host only; never commit them.
#OPENRUNG_RATE_LIMIT_RELAY_LIST=<rate>:<burst>
#OPENRUNG_RATE_LIMIT_WSS_TICKET=<rate>:<burst>
#OPENRUNG_RATE_LIMIT_USER_AUTH=<rate>:<burst>
#OPENRUNG_RATE_LIMIT_ACCESS=<rate>:<burst>
#OPENRUNG_RATE_LIMIT_TELEMETRY=<rate>:<burst>
#OPENRUNG_RATE_LIMIT_SPEED_TEST=<rate>:<burst>
#OPENRUNG_RATE_LIMIT_RELAY_REGISTRATION=<rate>:<burst>
# Relay-list page size when a request names none, and the largest a request
# may ask for (keep it at or above what shipped clients request).
#OPENRUNG_RELAY_LIST_DEFAULT_LIMIT=<n>
#OPENRUNG_RELAY_LIST_MAX_LIMIT=<n>
# Per-ticket WSS lifetime (2s-5m) and stream budget (1-1024).
#OPENRUNG_WSS_TICKET_TTL=<duration>
#OPENRUNG_WSS_TICKET_MAX_STREAMS=<n>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relay state store (optional; defaults to in-memory)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# memory (default) keeps relay state in the process; postgres shares it across
# restarts / multiple brokers behind a load balancer.
#OPENRUNG_RELAY_STORE=postgres
#OPENRUNG_RELAY_DATABASE_URL=postgres://openrung:change-me@db:5432/openrung?sslmode=disable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relay geolocation (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTP endpoint used to resolve a relay's public IP to city/country for the map.
# Set to "off" to disable outbound geo lookups entirely.
#OPENRUNG_GEOIP_ENDPOINT=off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relay reachability probe (optional; on by default)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The broker dials each direct relay's advertised public_host:public_port when
# it registers (and on heartbeat once the result is OPENRUNG_RELAY_PROBE_INTERVAL
# old) and withholds relays whose latest probe failed from clients. Set the
# first line to "off" to list every relay on its lease alone.
#OPENRUNG_RELAY_PROBE=off
#OPENRUNG_RELAY_PROBE_TIMEOUT=5s
#OPENRUNG_RELAY_PROBE_INTERVAL=5m

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telemetry storage (optional; defaults to the JSONL file)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# jsonl (default) appends events to OPENRUNG_TELEMETRY_FILE with zero
# dependencies; postgres writes them to a daily-partitioned telemetry_events
# table instead — recommended for production. The database URL falls back to
# OPENRUNG_RELAY_DATABASE_URL, so a broker already on the postgres relay store
# only needs the first line.
#OPENRUNG_TELEMETRY_STORE=postgres
#OPENRUNG_TELEMETRY_DATABASE_URL=postgres://openrung:change-me@db:5432/openrung?sslmode=disable

# The image points the JSONL file at the persistent named volume; override only
# if you change the volume mount in docker-compose.yml. Unused in postgres mode.
#OPENRUNG_TELEMETRY_FILE=/var/lib/openrung/telemetry.jsonl
